  "instructions": [
    {
      "primary_message": "This recipe will make about 2 cups of pesto. Work in batches to best fit your blender.\n\n",
      "secondary_message": null
    },
    {
      "primary_message": "Preheat a skillet over medium-high heat.\n\n",
      "secondary_message": null
    },
    {
      "primary_message": "Once the skillet is hot, add pine nuts and cook, stirring often, until golden and fragrant, about 3 minutes. Immediately transfer to the blender along with oil, salt, and pepper.\n\n",
      "secondary_message": "½ cup pine nuts\n¾ cup extra virgin olive oil\n½ tsp salt\n¼ tsp black pepper"
    },
    {
      "primary_message": "Wash, dry, and pick basil leaves off the stems. Add leaves to the blender.\n\n",
      "secondary_message": null
    },
    {
      "primary_message": "Wash, dry, and juice half of the lemon into the blender. (Reserve remaining lemon for the next step.)\n\n",
      "secondary_message": "1 lemon"
    },
    {
      "primary_message": "Peel garlic. Add to the blender, along with Parmesan, and blend on high, scraping down the sides of the blender as needed, until pesto is smooth. Taste, then adjust by adding additional lemon juice if desired. \n\n",
      "secondary_message": "4 clove garlic\n1 ½ cups Parmesan cheese, shredded"
    },
    {
      "primary_message": "STORING: Divide pesto between ½-cup containers, resealable plastic bags, or ice cube trays (according to preference). Freeze for up to 2 months. \n\n\n\nUSING: For best results, thaw pesto in the fridge overnight. Frozen pesto can also be added directly to simmering sauces, stews, and soups, stirring until melted.",
      "secondary_message": null
    }
  ],
